.. raw:: html

    <div class="sphx-glr-thumbcontainer" tooltip="Feynman&#x27;s path-integral formulation (Feynman &amp; Hibbs 1965, building on Dirac&#x27;s 1933 &quot;The Lagrangian in Quantum Mechanics&quot;) writes the quantum propagator as a sum over every continuous path x(t) connecting fixed endpoints, each weighted by a phase built from its classical action S[x(t)]. The same construction also applies to the harmonic oscillator, whose classical path curves rather than running straight between the fixed endpoints (~physicskit.semiclassical.core.path_integral.harmonic_oscillator_classical_path, harmonic_oscillator_classical_action), showing that the same closest-to-classical-first phasor concentration at small \hbar is not an artifact of the free particle&#x27;s trivially straight classical trajectory:">

